-- task-graph/hyperactivity.cub — this repository's task graph (lib/task-graph.cub; its meaning and rules
-- are docs/task-graph-design.md). The product, Avik's four goals and the one dependency between them
-- (2026-11-13). A goal with no children is one coarse task described by its statement; it is split
-- only when part of it is being tackled immediately (docs/task-graph-design.md §12). Known defects
-- and gaps found in the code are dated `finding (date):` comments at their sites.
--
-- finding (2026-11-12): question. Not yet done: reading the evaluator's rule bodies (src/net.c's
-- interaction cases, src/dnf.c's face algebra) side by side with the published rules, CCHM (Cohen,
-- Coquand, Huber, Mörtberg, TYPES 2015, LIPIcs 69) and the clocked extension (Kristensen,
-- Møgelberg, Vezzosi, arXiv:2102.01969), each rule recorded as matching or deviating at its site.
-- The two papers are not in the repository; fetch them. Until this is done, conformance of the
-- implementation to the calculus is not known.
--
-- finding (2026-11-12): question. Not yet done: reading src/lang.c's translation of terms into the
-- evaluator's graph for anything that can change a term's meaning (the strictness analysis that
-- decides which operands are built eagerly, fully lazy lifting, sweeping of compiled code, the
-- module cache, whose key is the build date and the files' texts and whose hits are trusted).
--
-- finding (2026-11-12): question. Not yet done: reading lib/check.cub's typing clauses against the
-- calculus's typing rules, each recorded as matching or deviating at its site.
--
import "../lib/task-graph.cub"
-- the-product (2026-11-13, Avik): "the product is every screened device in the world simply runs
-- hyperactive kernel. the term "application" becomes fully concrete - does not refer to a silo, but
-- literally to a particular application of computing." "*all* of this is to serve *product*." The
-- root: what Avik wants (docs/what-hyperactive-is-for-in-avik-words.md).
the-product : Node
the-product = node "The product: every screened device in the world runs Hyperactive." nil
-- language-implementation (2026-11-13, Avik): "complete implementation of hyperactive programming
-- language & features as specified by the paper ... get to the point i can show someone the paper
-- and correctly state "i have implemented this fully correctly" (obviously, that doesn't include
-- constructions about random examples about trading systems or message protocols)". The
-- initial high-priority deliverable.
language-implementation : Node
language-implementation = node "The Hyperactive language and its features, implemented completely and correctly as the paper specifies." (cons the-product nil)
-- paper-examples (2026-11-13, Avik): "term-by-term construction of every single construction
-- including examples, appendix, in paper, as code in repo cited by content-address in paper ...
-- expressely de-prioritized ... while this *serves nothing*." It serves no node but the root, so
-- the order of work gives it nothing while anything else is open.
paper-examples : Node
paper-examples = node "Every construction in the paper, its examples and appendix included, as a term in the repository, cited from the paper by content address." (cons the-product nil)
-- product-implementation (2026-11-13, Avik): "all the product work of building a live interactive
-- collaboration system & tooling over the language & its terms". How it is held, changed and shared:
-- docs/state-change-collaboration-design.md.
product-implementation : Node
product-implementation = node "The live interactive collaboration system and tooling over the language and its terms." (cons the-product nil)
-- language-support (2026-11-13, Avik): "Languages support (semantics/syntax as terms for particular
-- languages) * RISC x86, C++, C, Rust, OCaml, Python etc". "before we start any such work, certain
-- things need to be at perfection": it waits on language-implementation and product-implementation
-- (after, below).
language-support : Node
language-support = node "The syntax and semantics of particular languages as terms: x86, C, C++, Rust, OCaml, Python and others." (cons the-product nil)
-- calculus-conformance (2026-11-13, from the review of the findings of 2026-11-11/12): nobody has
-- compared the implementation with the published calculus, so whether it is "implemented fully
-- correctly" is unknown. Read the evaluator's rule cases (src/net.c), the translation of terms into
-- its graph (src/lang.c), the composition code generated per data type as unchecked text
-- (src/lang.c), universe composition (lib/prelude.cub, %hcompU, Cubical Agda's design, not CCHM's
-- Glue) and the checker's typing clauses (lib/check.cub) against CCHM (Cohen, Coquand, Huber,
-- Mörtberg) and the clocked extension (Kristensen, Møgelberg, Vezzosi), recording each rule as
-- matching or deviating, then fix or justify each deviation. The dated findings at those sites and in
-- this file's head say what is known. Large: the reading alone is days.
calculus-conformance : Node
calculus-conformance = node "The implementation is shown to be exactly the published calculus, rule by rule, and every deviation is fixed or justified." (cons language-implementation nil)
-- typed-codes (2026-11-13): paper §6.1. The language's terms as typed objects in it, normalization by
-- evaluation with its soundness, and a checker that returns typing derivations (lib/check.cub is an
-- untyped term with no statement of what it accepts; lib/std.cub's primitives give typed programs
-- its operations at asserted types). In progress in experiments/nbe/p26-wip.cub; what remains is in
-- lib/nbe.cub's head (F2d, F2d', F2e, fund, normalize, the swap), then Kan operations, Glue,
-- universes and object-level data types. The elaborator's two memory blowups met on the way are
-- findings in lib/check.cub and p26-wip. The longest chain of the project: it should never stop.
typed-codes : Node
typed-codes = node "The language's terms are typed objects in it, with normalization and its soundness, and the checker returns typing derivations (paper §6.1)." (cons language-implementation nil)
-- specializer (2026-11-13): paper §6. The specializer normalizes through a primitive that runs the
-- untyped checker, so it cannot be specialized itself; with normalization as a term (typed-codes) it
-- carries its law in its type and the three Futamura projections follow. Specializing a process
-- through a handler was measured superlinear (lib/specialize.cub, 2026-11-09) and is unattributed.
specializer : Node
specializer = node "The specializer carries its law in its type, and the three Futamura projections hold (paper §6)." (cons language-implementation nil)
-- native-through-c (2026-11-13): paper §9. Native code today comes from an x86 translator whose output
-- is bound and run with no relation to the definition (src/lang.c, lib/x86.cub), from pasted machine
-- code (lib/elf.cub), and with proofs about its bytes at one fixed placement (lib/compile/); nothing
-- relates a fleet's proved behaviour to its binaries (lib/deploy.cub). All of it is replaced by
-- realization through C whose type carries the relation between running the C and the term's
-- meaning. Large.
native-through-c : Node
native-through-c = node "Programs are realized as native code through C, and native code replaces a definition only with the relation to its meaning (paper §9)." (cons language-implementation nil)
-- primitive-laws (2026-11-13): lib/prim.cub postulates word and bit laws that are provable from the
-- unbounded definitions; fixed-width types have no specification; floating point is trusted
-- without a named assumption (paper §8.5). Medium and mechanical.
primitive-laws : Node
primitive-laws = node "Every law of the primitives is proved, or named as an assumption (paper §8.5)." (cons language-implementation nil)
-- typed-deriving (2026-11-13): lib/derive.cub's 24 definitions read the checker's own values and are
-- refused as untyped, so `deriving` is off and the Rust readers fail behind it. Deriving becomes a
-- term over the code of a data declaration, which needs a declaration's code as a value
-- (work-as-a-value).
typed-deriving : Node
typed-deriving = node "Deriving is a typed term over the code of a data declaration." (cons language-implementation nil)
-- deep-terms (2026-11-13): measured 2026-11-09, building a term cost quadratic time in its depth and
-- deep terms overflowed the C stack (src/net.c). Measured 2026-11-13 at depths 2,000, 4,000 and
-- 8,000: 2.6 s, 1.5 s, 4.0 s, so no longer quadratic there; the stack at depth 10^6 is not
-- re-measured. Small.
deep-terms : Node
deep-terms = node "The evaluator handles terms of any depth without quadratic cost or stack overflow." (cons language-implementation nil)
-- content-addressing (2026-11-13): definitions and results identified by content, the foundation of
-- collaboration with no canonical state (docs/state-change-collaboration-design.md). A reference is
-- keyed by its index in the run (src/net.c), so no result is kept or shared beyond a run
-- (src/lang.c); the checker's identity is a hash of file texts (src/store.c); the store writes in
-- place, so a killed machine leaves a truncated entry (src/store.c); the standard commands never use
-- the store (Makefile). Elaborations are already remembered (a warm library check, 4.6 s). It also
-- makes every check fast, which speeds all other work.
content-addressing : Node
content-addressing = node "Every definition and every result is identified by its content and kept, so work is shared across runs and collaborators." (cons product-implementation nil)
-- work-as-a-value (2026-11-13): serve mode gives views every declaration of a type as a value, but not
-- each declaration's code (src/lang.c); most declarations have no recorded reason (lib/task-graph.cub).
-- The interface for Avik (no editor, no IDE) reads the work through it.
work-as-a-value : Node
work-as-a-value = node "The work is a value of the language: every declaration's code and reason readable by views, and the interface reads it." (cons product-implementation nil)
-- documents that are essential context for working on a node and everything below it
-- (lib/task-graph.cub; docs/task-graph-design.md §7)
hyperactive-paper : Document
hyperactive-paper = document "../docs/Hyperactive_Final_Paper.pdf"
task-graph-design : Document
task-graph-design = document "../docs/task-graph-design.md"
state-change-collaboration-design : Document
state-change-collaboration-design = document "../docs/state-change-collaboration-design.md"
what-hyperactive-is-for-in-avik-words : Document
what-hyperactive-is-for-in-avik-words = document "../docs/what-hyperactive-is-for-in-avik-words.md"
essential-context : List (Node × List Document)
essential-context = cons (the-product , cons what-hyperactive-is-for-in-avik-words (cons hyperactive-paper (cons task-graph-design nil))) (cons (product-implementation , cons state-change-collaboration-design nil) nil)
-- the root: what Avik wants
roots : List Node
roots = cons the-product nil
-- language support starts only when the language implementation and the product implementation are
-- done (Avik, 2026-11-13); the specializer's law needs normalization as a term; typed deriving needs
-- a declaration's code as a value
after : List (Node × List Node)
after = cons (language-support , cons language-implementation (cons product-implementation nil)) (cons (specializer , cons typed-codes nil) (cons (typed-deriving , cons work-as-a-value nil) nil))
